<ServiceHero
  section="services"
  slug="refrigerator-repair/ge-ice-maker-not-working"
  eyebrow="GE Refrigerator Ice Maker · Diagnostic Guide · Same Day Southern California"
  title="GE Refrigerator Not Making Ice? Check the Feeler Arm First"
  subtitle="Most GE no-ice calls come down to the filter, supply, freezer temp — or a stuck feeler arm that tells the maker the bin is full. Here's what to check, GE's reset steps, and when it's a tech job."
  heroImageTitle="GE Ice Maker Not Working Repair"
  showBranches={true}
/>

<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · The short answer</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Work the water path — and free the feeler arm.</h2>

    <p><strong>When a GE refrigerator stops making ice, check the water path first: the filter (replace every six months — a clogged one starves the maker), the supply valve and line, a frozen fill tube, and the freezer temperature (around 0°F to cycle). Then check the feeler (bail) arm — on GE, a stuck or raised arm is the single most common cause, because it tells the ice maker the bin is full and stops production even when it's empty. Free the arm, make sure it's down, and run GE's reset (the side power switch off 15 seconds, or the recessed reset button). If the water path and arm are fine, the fault is the inlet valve or ice maker module — a tech job. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p>This page covers the ice maker built into your GE refrigerator. For a standalone or undercounter machine, use the <a href="/services/ice-maker-repair/">ice maker repair</a> hub; for fridge ice makers of any brand, the <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/ice-maker-issues/">refrigerator ice maker issues</a> guide is the umbrella.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 RESET (differentiator) -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · GE's reset steps</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Three ways to reset a GE ice maker.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Free and cycle the feeler arm</h3>
      <p>The wire feeler (bail) arm shuts the maker off when it rides up on a full bin. If it's jammed up by ice, frozen, or knocked off, the maker stops. Gently free it, push it up to off, wait, then pull it down to on to force a harvest.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Power switch off, then on</h3>
      <p>Find the power switch on the side or bottom of the ice maker, turn it off for at least 15 seconds, then back on. This clears many transient faults.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Recessed reset button</h3>
      <p>Many GE ice makers have a recessed reset button — remove the ice bucket to reach it and hold 3 to 5 seconds until a light flashes or the arm moves. Give it several hours for the first batch afterward.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Note: "Quick Ice" is not a reset</h3>
      <p>Quick Ice is a temporary production-boost feature, not a reset — turning it on won't restart a stopped ice maker. Use the steps above instead.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 CAUSES -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · Why a GE fridge ice maker stops</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The causes, in the order our techs find them.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Stuck or off feeler arm (most common on GE)</h3>
      <p>A jammed, frozen, or bumped-off feeler arm tells the maker the bin is full. Free it and set it down before assuming a part failed.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Clogged water filter</h3>
      <p>Good for about six months; past that, flow drops and ice tapers off then stops. Replacing an overdue filter is a top fix.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Closed supply, kinked line, or frozen fill tube</h3>
      <p>A partly-closed shutoff, a kinked line, or a frozen fill tube all cut off water to the mold. Confirm the supply and thaw the fill tube gently.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Water inlet valve or ice maker module</h3>
      <p>When water is available but the mold never fills, the inlet valve has failed; when the mechanism won't cycle, the module (motor, gears, thermostat behind the faceplate) is the fault. Both are tech diagnoses and replacements.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 SAFE FIX -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · What you can safely check</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Run these before calling anyone.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Free the feeler arm</strong> and make sure it's down in the on position.</li>
      <li><strong>Change the water filter</strong> if it's older than six months.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the supply</strong> — shutoff valve open, line not kinked — and thaw a frozen fill tube gently.</li>
      <li><strong>Verify the freezer temperature</strong> is around 0°F.</li>
      <li><strong>Reset it</strong> — cycle the arm, use the side power switch (off 15 seconds), or the recessed reset button. Give it several hours for the first batch.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Where to stop:</strong> freeing the arm, the filter, supply, fill-tube thaw, temperature, and a reset are safe homeowner work and fix most no-ice calls. Do not replace the water inlet valve or open the ice maker module — those are electrically live, plumbed-in tech jobs.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 TECH + LINKS -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · When it's a tech job</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Arm freed, water confirmed, still no ice? Now it's a diagnostic.</h2>

    <p>When the feeler arm moves freely, the filter is fresh, the supply is open, and a reset doesn't restore ice, the fault is the water inlet valve or the ice maker module. Our techs verify the water path and the arm, then test the valve and module before quoting — so you replace only the failed part. In LA's hard water we also clear mineral scale off the inlet-valve screen and fill tube, a common no-cost flow fix. <strong>$89 residential diagnostic, waived with the repair.</strong></p>

    <p>More help: the <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/ice-maker-issues/">refrigerator ice maker issues</a> umbrella (all brands), the full <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/">refrigerator repair</a> hub, and for a standalone unit, <a href="/services/ice-maker-repair/not-making-ice/">ice maker not making ice</a>. Brand coverage: <a href="/brands/ge-refrigerator-repair/">GE refrigerator repair</a> and <a href="/brands/ge-ice-maker-repair/">GE ice maker repair</a>. Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/refrigerator-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, <a href="/beverly-hills/refrigerator-repair/">Beverly Hills</a>, and <a href="/pasadena/refrigerator-repair/">Pasadena</a> in most cases. <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 FAQ -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">06 · FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">GE ice maker not working: common questions.</h2>

    <div class="faq-list">
